<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hlukhiv</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Glukhov</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hlukhiv is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumy">Sumy</a> Oblast in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. First mentioned in 1152 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> lands, it was the seat of a small principality of the Olgovichi, from which the princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novosil">Novosil</a> and the Upper Oka descended. From 1708 to 1764 it was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate</a> and the seat of the Little Russian Collegium.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hlukhiv.jpg" alt="Hlukhiv" width="330" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roman Naumov · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Глухівський_національний_педагогічний_університет_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hlukhiv, in Russian Glukhov, is a small historic city on the Esman River, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seym-river">Seym</a>, in Shostka Raion of Sumy Oblast. Its population was estimated at 31,789 in 2022. It is best known as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate</a> after the deposition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-mazepa">Ivan Mazepa</a> in 1708.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Chroniclers first noticed Hlukhiv in 1152 as a Severian town. Around 1247, after the Mongol invasion of Rus, it became the seat of a branch of the princely house of Chernihiv. From 1320 to 1503 it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, until conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a>. In 1618 it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-iv-vasa">Władysław IV Vasa</a> granted it Magdeburg Rights in 1644. From 1648 it was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate</a>, in the Nizhyn Regiment. In 1664 a Russo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> garrison defended it against a superior Polish army. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truce-of-andrusovo">Truce of Andrusovo</a> of 1667 it was ceded to Muscovy, and in 1669 Demian Mnohohrishny was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetman</a> there and concluded a treaty that significantly limited Ukrainian autonomy.</p><p>In 1708, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-mazepa">Ivan Mazepa</a> sided with Carl XII, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> ordered the destruction of Baturyn and the transfer of the capital to Hlukhiv. Ivan Skoropadsky was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetman</a> there in November 1708. Hlukhiv was the Hetmanate's capital until 1764 and until 1773 the administrative centre of the Little Russia Governorate. Under the last hetmans the town was remodelled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> style, but frequent fires later left few of its architectural treasures. After the Hetmanate was abolished it became the seat of the Second Little Russian Collegium; in 1782 it became a district centre of the Novgorod-Seversky Viceroyalty. The German army occupied it from 9 September 1941 to 30 August 1943.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The oldest building is the church of St. Nicholas of 1693, in Ukrainian Baroque style. The triumphal arch, dated to 1744 or 1766 and the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, was damaged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and restored. A singing school opened in 1730, the first in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, and the composers Dmytro Bortniansky and Maksym Berezovsky are believed to have studied there. Hlukhiv has long been a centre of hemp cultivation, and an institute of bast crops there bred low-THC hemp varieties in the 1970s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hlukhiv” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…by treaties: the March Articles of 1654, the Pereiaslav Articles of 1659 that opened the way to serfdom, the <b>Hlukhiv</b> Articles of 1669 and the Konotop Articles of 1672, which removed the right to an independent foreign policy and took the election of the hetman from ordinary Cossacks.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Semyon Mikhailovich received <b>Glukhov</b> and Novosil on the upper Oka, and Novosil became the senior seat of a group of small principalities known as the Upper Oka principalities.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-novosil">Principality of Novosil</a></li></ul></section>
